From c130852f05ebe32c4f2c1a9294f0f8cb9a1ac9f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 20:27:50 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] opcodes: microblaze: tell bslli, bsrai and bsrli apart

The immediate barrel shifts in opcodes/microblaze-opc.h are

	#define OPCODE_MASK_H3B   0xFC00F9E0 /* High 6 bits and bits 16:20 and
						bits 23:26. */
	{"bslli", ..., 0x64000400, OPCODE_MASK_H3B, bslli, ... },
	{"bsrai", ..., 0x64000200, OPCODE_MASK_H3B, bsrai, ... },
	{"bsrli", ..., 0x64000000, OPCODE_MASK_H3B, bsrli, ... },

The mask leaves out bits 21 and 22 (0x600), the only bits that tell the
three apart: 0x400 selects a left shift and 0x200 an arithmetic one.
The bit_sequence of bslli and bsrai therefore has bits outside its
opcode_mask, so the test "op->bit_sequence == (inst & op->opcode_mask)"
in read_insn_microblaze, get_insn_microblaze and
microblaze_get_target_address can never succeed for them.  Every
immediate barrel shift decodes as bsrli:

	   0:	64e4041f 	bsrli	r7, r4, 31	(bslli)
	   4:	64e4001f 	bsrli	r7, r4, 31
	   8:	64e4021f 	bsrli	r7, r4, 31	(bsrai)

gas encodes all three correctly.  It does not read opcode_mask, and the
object file it writes is byte-identical with and without this change.

The mask came in with commit 2d1777b530d ("opcodes: microblaze: Fix bit
masking bug"), first released in binutils 2.42.  Before that the three
entries used OPCODE_MASK_H3 (0xFC000600), which has bits 21:22 but none
of the bits that must be zero.

The simulator decodes through the same table: sim/microblaze/interp.c
calls get_insn_microblaze for each instruction.  A program that sets r4
to 1, runs "bslli r3, r4, 4" and exits with r3 exits with 0 under the
simulator built from master, and with 16 with this change.  With r4 set
to -32, "bsrai r3, r4, 28" exits with 15 before and 255 after.  GDB
disassembles through print_insn_microblaze and calls
get_insn_microblaze and microblaze_decode_insn from microblaze-tdep.c,
so it is affected as well; I have not built or run GDB.

The new mask, 0xFC00FFE0, is the high 6 bits and bits 16:26.  These are
the bits QEMU's target/microblaze/insns.decode fixes for bsrli, bsrai
and bslli.  OPCODE_MASK_H3B has no other users, so I changed its value
and comment rather than add a new macro.  The register forms bsll, bsra
and bsrl (OPCODE_MASK_H3) and bsefi and bsifi (OPCODE_MASK_H32B) already
include bits 21:22 and disassemble correctly.

I also checked every entry in microblaze_opcodes for bits outside its
own mask, and for an entry that an earlier one always matches.  With
this change the only hits are the pseudo-instructions whose instr is
invalid_inst (nop, la, not, neg, sub, lmi, smi, tuqula, rtb), which are
expected, and wdc.ext.clear: gas assembles "wdc.ext.clear r4, r5" as
0x90042c66 and objdump prints it as "wdc.clear r4, r5", because
wdc.clear comes first and OPCODE_MASK_H34B leaves out bit 21.  A
later patch in this series fixes that.

The new test gas/microblaze/bshift assembles bslli, bsrai and bsrli
with shift counts 31 and 3, and bsll, bsra and bsrl, and checks the
objdump -d output.  Without the opcodes change it fails on the four
bslli and bsrai lines; with it, it passes.

Tested on microblaze-elf, built on aarch64-apple-darwin, before and
after this change:

	gas:      331 -> 332 expected passes, 10 unsupported
	binutils: 245 expected passes, 2 expected failures, 18 untested,
	          17 unsupported, unchanged
	ld:       480 expected passes, 15 expected failures, 28 untested,
	          236 unsupported, unchanged

No unexpected failures in either run.  I have only tested microblaze-elf
and have not run the sim testsuite.

opcodes/
	* microblaze-opc.h (OPCODE_MASK_H3B): Include bits 21:22.

gas/
	* testsuite/gas/microblaze/bshift.exp: New file.
	* testsuite/gas/microblaze/bshift.s: New file.
	* testsuite/gas/microblaze/bshift.d: New test.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
